</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Singular Value Decomposition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Token covariance normalization ensures that the token embeddings are decorrelated or standardized by transforming the covariance matrix into an identity matrix.
This preprocessing step simplifies the structure of the embeddings, and ensures that the weight matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used on the normalized embeddings.
To use the normalized embeddings, OD-LLM applies SVD to the product <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{WS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{WS}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Cholesky factor derived from the covariance matrix of the original embeddings.
This approach ensures that SVD operates on the normalized feature space, which produces more efficient compression using a direct and interpretable mapping between singular values and the compression loss.
SVD on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{WS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{WS}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the decomposed matrices:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{WS}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐖𝐒</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{WS}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{r}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{r}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> = diag<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\mathbf{\sigma}_{1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{2},\mathbf{\sigma}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\mathbf{\sigma}_{1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{2},\mathbf{\sigma}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\mathbf{v}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{r}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\mathbf{v}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{r}]</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are the orthogonal matrices and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix containing the singular values <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{1}\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{2}\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{3}\geq\ldots\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{1}\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{2}\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{3}\geq\ldots\geq\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(M,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(M,N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To reduce the rank of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{WS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{WS}</annotation></semantics></math>, OD-LLM truncates smaller singular values in <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> below a certain threshold, and reconstructs the compressed weight matrix as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{U}\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})\mathbf{V}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Trunc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{U}\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})\mathbf{V}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The number of truncated values, i.e. the rank of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Trunc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rank=\left\lfloor\frac{M\cdot N\cdot CR}{M+N}\right\rfloor," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rank=\left\lfloor\frac{M\cdot N\cdot CR}{M+N}\right\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CR\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CR\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the compression ratio.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Reconstruction ensures that the compressed matrix is properly aligned with the original embedding space and preserves the most important components of <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So, the compression loss can be defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\|(\mathbf{W}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\|(\mathbf{W}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime})\mathbf{X}\|_{F},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the activation function of <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> given an input, and <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the compression loss in the form of the Frobenius loss.
A critical advantage of our approach is the direct mapping between the compression loss and truncated singular values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>)</cite>.
That is, when only one singular value <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is truncated, the compression loss is equal to the truncated singular value:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{r}=\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{r}=\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">When there is more than one singular values <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{rank+1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{rank+2},\ldots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{rank+1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{rank+2},\ldots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> truncated, the squared compression loss of the truncated multiple singular values is equal to the sum of their squares:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\sum_{i=rank+1}^{r}\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\sum_{i=rank+1}^{r}\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rank" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rank</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of retained singular values.
So, truncating the smallest singular values results in less compression loss.
By truncating the smallest singular values, the compression loss is minimized but still retains the most important features from the original weight matrix.
This ensures that compression is both efficient and preseves state.
The integration of the previous normalization and SVD ensures minimal compression loss and maximal efficiency in OD-LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we provide the proof of why the proposed normalization technique can provide a direct mapping between singular values and the compression loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 3.3.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Frobenius norm of a matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as the square root of the trace of the Gram matrix:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{F}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sum_{i=1}^{m}|a_{ij}|^{2}}=\sqrt{\text{trace}(A^{T}A)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{F}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\sum_{i=1}^{m}|a_{ij}|^{2}}=\sqrt{\text{trace}(A^{T}A)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Using Lemma 3.3, we can derive a compression loss <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> when truncating the <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th singular value of <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to reduce the rank:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|(\mathbf{W}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|(\mathbf{W}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3},...,\mathbf{u}_{r}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3},...,\mathbf{u}_{r}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\mathbf{v}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{r}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\mathbf{v}_{3},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{r}]</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal matrices, we have:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}=\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{i}=I,\quad\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{v}_{j}=\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{j}=0,\quad\forall i\neq j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}=\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{i}=I,\quad\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{v}_{j}=\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{j}=0,\quad\forall i\neq j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p18.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{trace}(\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T})=\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{trace}(\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T})=\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p18.3" class="ltx_p">Since the covariance normalization matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Cholesky decomposition of <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}\mathbf{S}^{T}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒𝐒</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}\mathbf{S}^{T}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Equation (15).
This can be simplified to:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}\sqrt{\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{\sigma}_{i};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the loss <math id="S3.SS4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> incurred by truncating <math id="S3.SS4.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the singular value <math id="S3.SS4.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> itself, which proves Equation (11) is true.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p20.1" class="ltx_p">If we truncate multiple singular values <math id="S3.SS4.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{m+1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{m+2},\dots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{m+1},\mathbf{\sigma}_{m+2},\dots,\mathbf{\sigma}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS4.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, the squared compression loss <math id="S3.SS4.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p21" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}=\left\|\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\right\|_{F}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}=\left\|\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\right\|_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p22.1" class="ltx_p">which can be expanded to:</p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sum_{j=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{u}_{j}^{T})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sum_{j=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^{T}(\mathbf{S}^{-1})^{T}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{u}_{j}^{T}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Since the orthogonal properties of <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> ensure <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{T})\\
=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{u}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{T})\\
=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, this can then be simplified to:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p24" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}^{2}\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>trace</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}^{2}\text{trace}(\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{T})=\sum_{i=m+1}^{r}\sigma_{i}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the squared loss <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the sum of the squared singular values, which is consistent with Equation (12).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Progressive Weight Matrix Updates</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When using SVD to compress a weight matrix, truncating smaller singular values can lead to discrepancies between the original and the compressed model.
Specifically, as the compression ratio increases, the compressed weight matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> deviates from the original weight matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> more, causing the activations produced to differ from those produced by <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To address this problem, we selectively update the weight matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> layerwise, ensuring that the compressed model retains the ability to generate activations which are similar to the original model, thereby minimizing the performance loss introduced by SVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>)</cite>.
So, the key innovation is to refine the compressed model by updating each layer independently, using the activations generated in the previous layer.
Rather than globally updating the entire weight matrix after compression, which may lead to lower quality compressed models, we propose a more principled update process.
The weight matrix in each layer is adjusted to reduce the discrepancy between the activations produced by the original and the compressed model.
The update is performed only on the matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each layer <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents the left singular vectors of the decomposed weight matrix.
The remaining components, such as <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the truncated singular values in <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, are fixed to preserve the low-rank structure of the model.
When given the same calibration data as input, the compressed weight matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a new activation <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that differs from <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is generated from the original weight matrix <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{W}_{i}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}_{i-1}\mathbf{D}^{T}(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{D}^{T})^{-1},\\
\quad\mathbf{D}=\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})_{i}\mathbf{V}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}_{i}^{-1}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}_{i-1};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐃𝐃</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Trunc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{W}_{i}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}_{i-1}\mathbf{D}^{T}(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{D}^{T})^{-1},\\
\quad\mathbf{D}=\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})_{i}\mathbf{V}_{i}^{T}\mathbf{S}_{i}^{-1}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}_{i-1};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Trunc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Trunc}(\mathbf{\Sigma})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the truncated singular value matrix for layer <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
After each progressive layer update, we expect improved performance even at higher compression ratios.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>)</cite>, we evaluate our method using three real-world recommender systems datasets from Amazon review data <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/</a></span></span></span>: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Instruments</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Games</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arts</span>.
Each item in the dataset is associated with a title and a description.
For any sequence <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}=\{v_{1}^{u},v_{2}^{u},\ldots,v_{\ell}^{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}=\{v_{1}^{u},v_{2}^{u},\ldots,v_{\ell}^{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the last two interacting items, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\ell}^{u},v_{\ell-1}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\ell}^{u},v_{\ell-1}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, are used as the validation and test set respectively.
A summary of important data statistics is presented in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3. Token Covariance Normalization ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ On-Device Large Language Models for Sequential Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first compare our method with a few representative traditional sequential recommendation models and then compare our results using a few small language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Caser</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>)</cite> is a novel top-N sequential recommendation system that models recent actions as an ‘image of time and latent dimensions, and learns sequential patterns using convolutional filters to capture point-level and union-level sequential patterns, skipwise behaviors, and long term user preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HGN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>)</cite> is a hierarchical gating network (HGN), ingegrated with Bayesian Personalized Ranking (BPR) to capture both the long-term and short-term user interests.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GRU4Rec</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>)</cite> proposes an RNN-based approach to model the whole sequence of session-based recommendations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bert4Rec</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>)</cite> uses deep bidirectional self-attention to model user behavior sequences to learn a bidirectional representation model, and makes recommendations by allowing each item in user behavior history to combine information bidirectionally.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FDSA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>)</cite> first integrates various heterogeneous features for items into feature sequences and separates self-attention blocks based on item-level sequences and feature-level sequences to model the item transition patterns and the feature transition patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SASRec</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>)</cite> is a Transformer-based sequential recommendation model that captures long-term semantics and makes predictions using relatively few input actions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S3-Rec</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>)</cite> employs mutual information maximization to pre-train a self-supervised sequential recommendation model, capturing the relationships between items and the associated attributes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TIGER</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>)</cite> adopts a generative retrieval approach to sequential recommendation by introducing semantic IDs to uniquely represent items.
As the authors have not released the official source code, we re-implement the model using Transformers-3, based on the implementation details outlined in the paper.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LC-Rec</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>)</cite>: we compare our compressed model using the original LC-Rec model.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since our method is designed for sequential recommendation tasks, we use Hit Ratio and NDCG (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain) to evaluate the recommendation results of the top-5 and top-10 results.
The Hit Ratio measures the ratio of correct items predicted and NDCG uses a weighted gain function based on the rank position of the relevant items in the recommendation list.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>Training Settings</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the LLM-based baselines, we also use a similar fine-tuning strategy for our recommendation datasets.
When training the LLaMA model, we follow the settings from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>)</cite>. For the compression setting, the compression ratio is set to 0.5; the number of calibration samples is 256; the model sequence length is set to 200; the evaluation batch size is 1 and the number of beans is 20. The whole method is done on a single A40 GPU.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2601.09306v1/figure2.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="238" height="182"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Ablation Study.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Overall Performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of our proposed method, we conduct experiments using three datasets, and compare the performance against traditional sequential recommendation baselines and also the uncompressed backbone model LC-Rec.
For simplicity, our method represents the model where each weight matrix has a 50% compression ratio. The results are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3. Token Covariance Normalization ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ On-Device Large Language Models for Sequential Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional sequential models such as SASRec, GRU4Rec, and HGN are reasonably effective, with GRU4Rec being the best performing baseline for NDCG, indicating better ranking capabilities.
Bert4Rec and Caser perform poorly in most settings, suggesting that their architectural choices may be less effective at capturing complex user-item interactions.
Among all baselines, LC-Rec demonstrates the best overall performance across the three datasets, achieving the highest scores for all evaluation metrics considered, which highlights its capacity to integrate collaborative semantics into sequential recommendation.
Across all three datasets, OD-LLM performs well, or even surpasses, the strongest baseline (LC-Rec).
On the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Instruments</span> dataset, the two best methods are essentially indistinguishable for the highest ranks, with OD-LLM having a modest advantage at deeper cutoffs.
In the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Games</span> dataset, the relative ranking patterns differ: OD-LLM demonstrates stronger performance at deeper ranks, whereas LC-Rec shows a slight advantage in the highest-ranked results.
In the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arts</span> dataset, OD-LLM consistently outperforms LC-Rec across all evaluation metrics, achieving substantial relative improvements.
Recall that LC-Rec is the uncompressed model, whereas OD-LLM can achieve these results using roughly half the model size to deliver similar or even better ranking quality with a significantly smaller memory footprint.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2601.09306v1/figure3.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="238" height="198"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Impact of Compression Ratio.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Model</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">HR@5</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">NDCG@5</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">HR@10</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">NDCG@10</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">GPTQ</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.1020</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.0880</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.1232</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.0951</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">SparseGPT</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.0980</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.0830</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.1200</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0905</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">OD-LLM</td>
